<section id="A1.SS1.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">MIA evaluation.</h5>

<div id="A1.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For sample-level evaluation, we randomly sample <math id="A1.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="10{,}000" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>10,000</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">10{,}000</annotation></semantics></math> member transition samples and <math id="A1.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="10{,}000" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>10,000</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">10{,}000</annotation></semantics></math> non-member transition samples from the training and reserved splits, respectively.
For trajectory-level evaluation, we sample <math id="A1.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="100" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>100</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">100</annotation></semantics></math> member trajectories and <math id="A1.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="100" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>100</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">100</annotation></semantics></math> non-member trajectories.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A1.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">A.2 </span>Additional Results on <math id="A1.SS2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{0.5}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mn>0.5</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{0.5}</annotation></semantics></math></h3>

<div id="A1.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To evaluate whether membership leakage persists across different VLA architectures, we additionally report results on <math id="A1.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{0.5}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mn>0.5</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{0.5}</annotation></semantics></math>, which augments the backbone with an action expert module.
Since <math id="A1.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{0.5}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mn>0.5</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{0.5}</annotation></semantics></math> uses a flow-matching-based action generation mechanism, we adapt the likelihood-based attack by using Flow-Loss as the corresponding training-loss signal.
We also evaluate action-based and trajectory-level attacks following the same protocol as in the main experiments.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Table <a href="#A1.T5" title="Table 5 ‣ A.2 Additional Results on 𝜋_0.5 ‣ Appendix A Appendix ‣ Membership Inference Attacks on Vision-Language-Action Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a> shows that <math id="A1.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{0.5}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mn>0.5</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{0.5}</annotation></semantics></math> also exhibits strong membership leakage.
At the sample level, Action-L1 achieves high AUCs across all four datasets, ranging from <math id="A1.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.9009" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.9009</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.9009</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="A1.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.9481" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.9481</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.9481</annotation></semantics></math>. At the trajectory level, leakage becomes nearly perfect.
Action-L1 and Agg.-Flow-Loss achieve AUCs close to or equal to <math id="A1.SS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1.0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1.0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1.0</annotation></semantics></math> across all datasets, demonstrating that trajectory-level aggregation strongly amplifies membership signals. Overall, these results confirm that membership leakage is not specific to a single VLA architecture.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A1.T5" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering" style="font-size:70%;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table"><span id="A1.T5.6" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:129%;">Table 5</span>: </span><span id="A1.T5.7" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:129%;">MIA results for <math id="A1.T5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{0.5}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mn>0.5</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{0.5}</annotation></semantics></math> across four LIBERO datasets. Best result within each suite-level block is bolded.</span></figcaption>
